.TH NWM 1 nwm\-VERSION
.SH NAME
nwm \- nano window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nwm
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
nwm is a minimal tiling window manager for X11. It manages windows in
three layouts (tiling, floating and monocle), groups them with tags and
draws nothing except window borders. It has no built-in bar; an external
EWMH-aware bar can be used instead (see
.BR "EXTERNAL BARS" ).
.P
nwm is configured at compile time only. There is no configuration file,
no IPC and no reload mechanism. See
.BR CUSTOMIZATION .
.P
nwm manages the whole X screen as a single monitor. It does not start any
other programs by itself; start background programs from
.I ~/.xinitrc
before the
.B exec nwm
line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
Print version information to standard output and exit.
.P
Any other argument prints a usage message to standard error and exits.
.SH USAGE
Add the following line as the last command of
.IR ~/.xinitrc :
.P
.RS
.EX
exec nwm
.EE
.RE
.P
For a display manager, install a session file such as
.IR /usr/share/xsessions/nwm.desktop .
.SS Tags
nwm has one tag per entry of
.B tags[]
(nine by default, at most 31). Every window carries a tag bitmask and the
current view is a bitmask. A window is visible when its tags and the
current view have at least one tag in common, so one window can be shown
on several tags, and several tags can be viewed at once.
.P
.B Mod+Tab
returns to the previously saved view, including combined multi-tag views.
It is not simply "the previous tag": nwm keeps two view slots and flips
between them on each view change.
.SS Layouts
.TP
.B tiling
Master and stack areas. The number of master windows and the master area
size can be changed at run time. Floating windows are not tiled.
.TP
.B floating
Windows keep their own geometry. Gaps are not applied.
.TP
.B monocle
Every visible window fills the work area; the focused one is on top.
Gaps are applied.
.P
The layout, master factor and master count are stored per tag unless
.B NWM_WITH_PERTAG
is set to 0. When several tags are viewed, the state of the lowest
numbered visible tag is used.
.SS Floating windows
Dialog windows (\fB_NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DIALOG\fR), windows that request
fullscreen and windows matched by a floating rule are floating. A floating
window respects ICCCM size hints. A focused floating window keeps focus
when the pointer moves over tiled windows. Moving or resizing a tiled
window by more than
.B snap
pixels makes it floating.
.SS Fullscreen
A fullscreen window covers the whole screen, including a bar. While it is
focused,
.B Mod+j
and
.B Mod+k
and focus-follows-mouse do not change focus.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
The default modifier
.B Mod
is the Super (Windows) key. Change
.B MODKEY
in
.I nwm.h
to use another one, for example
.B Mod1Mask
for Alt.
.SS Windows and layouts
.TP
.B Mod+Return
Spawn a terminal (default:
.BR st (1)).
.TP
.B Mod+d
Spawn a launcher (default:
.BR dmenu_run (1)).
.TP
.B Mod+j
Focus the next window. In tiling layouts floating windows are skipped.
.TP
.B Mod+k
Focus the previous window. In tiling layouts floating windows are skipped.
.TP
.B Mod+h
Shrink the master area by 5%.
.TP
.B Mod+l
Grow the master area by 5%.
.TP
.B Mod+i
Increase the number of master windows.
.TP
.B Mod+o
Decrease the number of master windows.
.TP
.B Mod+Space
Promote the focused window to master. If it already is the master, the
next tiled window is promoted instead.
.TP
.B Mod+t
Select the tiling layout.
.TP
.B Mod+f
Select the floating layout.
.TP
.B Mod+m
Select the monocle layout.
.TP
.B Mod+F11
Toggle fullscreen for the focused window.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+Space
Toggle floating for the focused window.
.TP
.B Mod+q
Close the focused window. nwm sends
.B WM_DELETE_WINDOW
if the client supports it and kills the client otherwise.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+e
Quit nwm.
.SS Tags
.TP
.B Mod+1\-9
View tag
.IR N .
.TP
.B Mod+Ctrl+1\-9
Toggle tag
.I N
in the current view.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+1\-9
Move the focused window to tag
.IR N .
.TP
.B Mod+Ctrl+Shift+1\-9
Toggle tag
.I N
on the focused window.
.TP
.B Mod+0
View all tags.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+0
Put the focused window on all tags.
.TP
.B Mod+Tab
Return to the previous view.
.SS Mouse
These apply when the modifier is held over a client window.
.TP
.B Mod+Button1
Move the window.
.TP
.B Mod+Button2
Toggle floating.
.TP
.B Mod+Button3
Resize the window. The pointer is moved to the bottom-right corner of
the window.
.P
While moving a window with
.BR Mod+Button1 ,
pressing
.B Mod+1\-9
carries the window to that tag and switches the view to it; the drag
continues, so the window can be dropped there or carried further.
.B Mod+0
sends the window to all tags. Other bindings are ignored during a drag.
This reuses the
.B view
bindings from
.BR keys[] ,
so rebinding them rebinds this too.
.P
All bindings are defined in the
.B keys[]
and
.B buttons[]
arrays in
.IR nwm.h .
.SH EXTERNAL BARS
nwm draws no bar and has no status text. It cooperates with external
bars (for example
.BR polybar (1),
.BR tint2 (1)
or
.BR lemonbar (1))
through standard EWMH hints, so a bar needs no support code in nwm and
can be started before or after nwm.
.SS Docks and reserved space
A window of type
.B _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DOCK
is not a client: nwm maps it as it is and gives it no border, tag or
focus handling. The screen edges it reserves with
.B _NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL
(or the older
.BR _NET_WM_STRUT )
are removed from the work area. Tiling, monocle, new floating windows and
drag snapping use the area that is left, and nwm publishes it as
.BR _NET_WORKAREA .
.P
The work area is recalculated when a dock appears or disappears and when
its strut property changes. Details:
.IP \(bu 2
Per edge the largest strut wins; struts of several docks are not added up.
.IP \(bu 2
Strut start and end ranges are ignored, because the whole X screen is one
monitor.
.IP \(bu 2
At most 8 docks are tracked (\fBdocks[8]\fR in
.IR nwm.c ).
Further docks are mapped but reserve no space.
.IP \(bu 2
Fullscreen windows still cover the whole screen, bar included.
.IP \(bu 2
A panel that does not set the dock type is managed as an ordinary window.
A bar that is override-redirect is never seen by nwm: nothing is
reserved and it may overlap windows.
.SS Tags as desktops
Every tag is an EWMH desktop, so a bar can show tag names, the current tag
and occupied tags:
.TP
.B _NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS
The number of entries in
.BR tags[] .
.TP
.B _NET_DESKTOP_NAMES
The names from
.BR tags[] .
.TP
.B _NET_DESKTOP_GEOMETRY, _NET_DESKTOP_VIEWPORT
Published for completeness; desktops are not larger than the screen and
the viewport is always 0,0.
.TP
.B _NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP
The lowest tag of the current view.
.TP
.B _NET_WM_DESKTOP
Set on every client: the lowest tag of the window, or 0xFFFFFFFF if the
window is on all tags.
.TP
.B _NET_CLIENT_LIST, _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW
The managed windows and the focused one, for taskbars and title widgets.
.P
These properties are written only when their value changes, and are
removed when nwm exits.
.SS What a bar can do
.IP \(bu 2
Switch tags: send
.B _NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP
to the root window. The view becomes that single tag; an index outside
the range of
.B tags[]
is ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
Activate a window (for example a click in a taskbar): this only marks the
window urgent, so its border gets the urgent color. nwm does not move
focus on such requests, to avoid focus stealing.
.IP \(bu 2
It cannot move windows between tags: the
.B _NET_WM_DESKTOP
client message is not handled.
.SS Setting up a bar
Start the bar from
.I ~/.xinitrc
(nwm has no autostart):
.P
.RS
.EX
polybar main &
exec nwm
.EE
.RE
.P
For
.BR polybar (1)
use a normal window,
.B override-redirect = false
in the bar section, and the
.B internal/xworkspaces
and
.B internal/xwindow
modules with
.BR "enable-click = true" .
For
.BR lemonbar (1)
pass
.B \-d
so that the bar is a docked window. Tag names come from
.BR tags[] ;
recompile to rename them, for example to
.IR web ,
.IR dev
and
.IR mail .
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
nwm is customized by editing
.I nwm.h
and recompiling:
.P
.RS
.EX
$ $EDITOR nwm.h
$ make clean install
.EE
.RE
.P
Then restart nwm. The most important options are:
.TP
.B MODKEY
Modifier key used by all default bindings.
.TP
.B borderpx
Border width in pixels.
.TP
.B col_nborder, col_sborder, col_uborder
Border colors of inactive, focused and urgent windows.
.TP
.B gappx
Gap between windows and screen edges.
.TP
.B mfact, nmaster
Initial master area ratio (0.05\-0.95) and number of master windows.
.TP
.B snap
Snap and float-on-drag threshold in pixels.
.TP
.B attachbottom
If 1, new windows are appended to the end of the client list.
.TP
.B focusonopen
If 0, a new window does not take focus.
.TP
.B tags[]
Tag names; the number of entries is the number of tags.
.TP
.B termcmd, dmenucmd
Commands started by
.B Mod+Return
and
.BR Mod+d .
.TP
.B NWM_WITH_BORDERS, NWM_WITH_GAPS, NWM_WITH_PERTAG
Set to 0 to remove the corresponding code and data from the build.
.SS Window rules
Rules are listed in the
.B rules[]
array in
.IR nwm.h .
Each rule has the fields
.IR class ,
.IR instance ,
.I title
(a substring of the window title),
.I tags
(a tag bitmask, 0 keeps the current tags) and
.I isfloating
(1 floating, 0 tiled, \-1 default). Only the first matching rule is
applied. Dialog and fullscreen windows are made floating after the
rules are applied.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X server to connect to.
.SH EXIT STATUS
nwm exits with 0 when it is quit normally (for example with
.BR Mod+Shift+e ).
It exits with a non-zero status if it cannot open the display, if another
window manager is already running, or on a fatal error.
.SH STANDARDS
nwm implements parts of the ICCCM and EWMH specifications:
.BR WM_DELETE_WINDOW ,
.BR WM_TAKE_FOCUS ,
.BR WM_NORMAL_HINTS ,
.BR WM_HINTS ,
.BR WM_STATE ,
.B _NET_SUPPORTED
and the other hints named above. On OpenBSD nwm uses
.BR pledge (2).
.SH BUGS AND LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
The screen size is read once at startup. RandR, multiple monitors and
resolution changes are not handled.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no minimize or iconify support.
.IP \(bu 2
Changes to
.B _NET_WM_STATE
after a window is mapped are not tracked; state changes made through
client messages are honored.
.IP \(bu 2
A request to activate a window
.RB ( _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW )
only marks it urgent and does not move focus. The
.B _NET_WM_DESKTOP
client message is not handled, so a bar cannot move windows between tags.
.IP \(bu 2
.B _NET_CLIENT_LIST_STACKING
and
.B _NET_SHOWING_DESKTOP
are not implemented.
.P
Report bugs at
.IR https://github.com/tinyopsec/nwm/issues .
